---
export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    {
      params: { slug: 'how-to-study-smart-for-usmle-step-1' },
      props: {
        title: "How to Study Smart for USMLE Step 1 in 2025",
        date: "May 15, 2025",
        author: "Dr. Sarah Johnson",
        authorTitle: "USMLE Step 1 Score: 270",
        category: "USMLE Step 1",
        image: "https://images.pexels.com/photos/3769021/pexels-photo-3769021.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1260&h=750&dpr=2",
        content: `
        <p class="lead">With USMLE Step 1 transitioning to pass/fail, many students wonder how this changes their study approach. While the pressure of achieving a high score is gone, passing remains crucial, and developing a strong foundation is still essential for Step 2 CK success.</p>
        
        <h2>Understanding the New Pass/Fail System</h2>
        <p>The USMLE Step 1 is now scored as pass/fail only, with a passing score set at 196. Despite this change, the content and difficulty level remain the same, requiring a comprehensive understanding of basic sciences and their clinical applications.</p>
        
        <h2>Key Study Strategies for 2025</h2>
        <p>Based on our analysis of the most recent exam patterns and student experiences, here are the most effective strategies for Step 1 preparation:</p>
        
        <h3>1. Focus on High-Yield Topics</h3>
        <p>Our recall analysis shows these concepts appear most frequently:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Biochemistry: metabolic pathways, vitamin deficiencies</li>
          <li>Pathology: inflammation, neoplasia mechanisms</li>
          <li>Pharmacology: autonomic drugs, antineoplastics</li>
          <li>Physiology: renal, cardiovascular, respiratory</li>
          <li>Microbiology: clinically relevant pathogens</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h3>2. Integrate Basic Sciences with Clinical Applications</h3>
        <p>The exam increasingly focuses on the clinical relevance of basic science concepts. Study with clinical vignettes that force you to apply basic science knowledge to clinical scenarios.</p>
        
        <h3>3. Use Active Learning Techniques</h3>
        <p>Research shows active recall is far more effective than passive reviewing. Implement these techniques:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Question banks (aim for at least 3,000 practice questions)</li>
          <li>Flashcards for spaced repetition</li>
          <li>Teaching concepts to others</li>
          <li>Creating concept maps</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h3>4. Optimize Your Study Resources</h3>
        <p>Our survey of successful students revealed these top resources:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Question Banks: UWorld, Amboss</li>
          <li>Review Books: First Aid, Pathoma</li>
          <li>Videos: Boards & Beyond, Sketchy</li>
          <li>Flashcards: Anki</li>
          <li>Comprehensive AI-powered Recalls: MedRecalls Premium Step 1 Package</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h2>Sample 8-Week Study Plan</h2>
        <p>Here's an efficient 8-week study schedule based on our analysis of successful study patterns:</p>
        
        <h3>Weeks 1-2: Foundation Building</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Complete first pass of First Aid</li>
          <li>Begin content review with videos</li>
          <li>Start UWorld on tutor mode by system</li>
          <li>Review MedRecalls high-yield concepts</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h3>Weeks 3-5: Comprehensive Review</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Continue UWorld questions</li>
          <li>Targeted review of weak areas</li>
          <li>Pathoma complete review</li>
          <li>Anki review of high-yield facts</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h3>Weeks 6-7: Integration and Practice</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Begin UWorld second pass or new qbank</li>
          <li>NBME practice exams</li>
          <li>Review MedRecalls exam predictions</li>
          <li>Focus on weak areas identified in practice tests</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h3>Week 8: Final Review</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>High-yield review of First Aid</li>
          <li>UWSA and Free 120</li>
          <li>Review MedRecalls' most recent updates</li>
          <li>Relax the day before the exam</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h2>Recent Exam Trends (2025)</h2>
        <p>Based on our analysis of the most recent exam recalls, we're seeing these trends:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Increased focus on genetics and molecular mechanisms</li>
          <li>More questions on quality improvement and patient safety</li>
          <li>Greater emphasis on pharmacokinetics and drug interactions</li>
          <li>More complex, multi-step pathophysiology questions</li>
          <li>Questions requiring interpretation of lab results and imaging</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h2>Test Day Strategies</h2>
        <p>Even with the pass/fail system, optimizing your performance on test day remains important:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Take breaks strategically to maintain concentration</li>
          <li>Use a consistent approach for answering questions</li>
          <li>Don't change answers unless you have a clear reason</li>
          <li>Skip difficult questions and return to them later</li>
          <li>Remember that some questions are experimental and won't count toward your score</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
        <p>While Step 1 is now pass/fail, developing a strong foundation remains critical for clinical success and Step 2 CK performance. Focus on understanding concepts rather than memorization, and use high-yield resources to maximize your efficiency.</p>
        
        <p>With the right approach and resources, you can confidently pass Step 1 and build the knowledge foundation needed for clinical rotations and Step 2 CK.</p>
        
        <div class="cta-box">
          <h3>Ready for Comprehensive Step 1 Preparation?</h3>
          <p>Get our AI-powered Step 1 recall package featuring verified high-yield content from recent exams, comprehensive explanations, and weekly updates.</p>
          <a href="https://gumroad.com" class="btn-accent">Get Step 1 Premium Package</a>
        </div>
        `
      }
    },
    {
      params: { slug: 'high-yield-topics-step-2-ck' },
      props: {
        title: "Top 10 High-Yield Topics for Step 2 CK in 2025",
        date: "May 10, 2025",
        author: "Dr. Michael Chen",
        authorTitle: "USMLE Step 2 CK Score: 275",
        category: "USMLE Step 2 CK",
        image: "https://images.pexels.com/photos/4031818/pexels-photo-4031818.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1260&h=750&dpr=2",
        content: `
        <p class="lead">As Step 1 transitions to pass/fail, Step 2 CK has become increasingly important for residency applications. Understanding the highest-yield topics can significantly improve your score and enhance your residency prospects.</p>
        
        <h2>The Changing Landscape of Step 2 CK</h2>
        <p>The 2025 Step 2 CK exam continues to evolve, with greater emphasis on clinical reasoning, patient management, and systems-based practice. Our analysis of recent exams reveals clear patterns in the most frequently tested content.</p>
        
        <h2>Top 10 High-Yield Topics for 2025</h2>
        
        <h3>1. Preventive Medicine & Screening Guidelines</h3>
        <p>Recent exams heavily test USPSTF screening recommendations, vaccination schedules, and preventive health measures. Know the specific age ranges, risk factors, and indications for common screenings (mammography, colonoscopy, PSA, etc.) and all CDC-recommended vaccines.</p>
        
        <h3>2. Obstetrics & Gynecology Emergencies</h3>
        <p>Questions frequently focus on pregnancy complications, including preeclampsia/eclampsia, HELLP syndrome, postpartum hemorrhage, ectopic pregnancy, and placental abnormalities. Management of these emergencies consistently appears in numerous vignettes.</p>
        
        <h3>3. Pediatric Developmental Milestones & Disorders</h3>
        <p>Developmental delays, autism spectrum disorders, ADHD, and normal developmental milestones are heavily tested. Questions often require identifying appropriately timed interventions and differentiating normal from abnormal development.</p>
        
        <h3>4. Biostatistics & Evidence-Based Medicine</h3>
        <p>Step 2 CK now includes more complex biostatistics questions, including study design, statistical significance, relative risk, odds ratios, and interpretation of medical literature. These concepts frequently appear in dedicated questions and within clinical vignettes.</p>
        
        <h3>5. Acute Management of Cardiovascular Conditions</h3>
        <p>ACS, heart failure exacerbations, arrhythmias, and hypertensive emergencies are extensively tested. Focus on immediate management steps, medication selection, and appropriate follow-up care for these conditions.</p>
        
        <h3>6. Psychiatric Emergencies & Management</h3>
        <p>Recent exams show increased testing of psychiatric emergencies, including suicidal ideation, psychosis, and severe mood disorders. Questions focus on immediate safety measures, pharmacological interventions, and appropriate disposition.</p>
        
        <h3>7. Quality Improvement & Patient Safety</h3>
        <p>This emerging area now comprises 5-10% of the exam, focusing on error prevention, reporting mechanisms, quality improvement methodologies, and systems-based approaches to enhancing patient care.</p>
        
        <h3>8. Antimicrobial Stewardship & Infectious Disease Management</h3>
        <p>Step 2 CK increasingly tests appropriate antimicrobial selection, de-escalation strategies, and management of common infections. Know empiric therapy choices for common presentations and when to narrow coverage based on culture results.</p>
        
        <h3>9. Geriatric Medicine</h3>
        <p>With the aging population, geriatric syndromes are heavily tested, including fall risk assessment, polypharmacy management, dementia vs. delirium, and end-of-life care discussions.</p>
        
        <h3>10. Ethical & Legal Aspects of Medicine</h3>
        <p>Questions on informed consent, decision-making capacity, advance directives, and confidentiality appear frequently. Recent exams emphasize resource allocation, medical errors, and disclosure of adverse events.</p>
        
        <h2>Topic Breakdown by Percentage</h2>
        <p>Based on our analysis of recent exam recalls, here's the approximate distribution of questions:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Internal Medicine: 25-30%</li>
          <li>Surgery: 15-20%</li>
          <li>Pediatrics: 15-20%</li>
          <li>Obstetrics & Gynecology: 15%</li>
          <li>Psychiatry: 10%</li>
          <li>Quality Improvement & Patient Safety: 5-10%</li>
          <li>Biostatistics & Evidence-Based Medicine: 5%</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h2>Effective Study Strategies</h2>
        <p>To master these high-yield topics, we recommend the following approach:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Focus on clinical vignettes and active question practice</li>
          <li>Prioritize understanding management decisions over pure recall</li>
          <li>Use clinical algorithms and decision trees</li>
          <li>Practice with timed blocks of questions</li>
          <li>Incorporate MedRecalls' Step 2 CK question bank, which is specifically designed to reflect current exam patterns</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h2>Resources That Align with Current Exam Patterns</h2>
        <p>Our survey of top-scoring students found these resources to be most valuable:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Question Banks: UWorld Step 2 CK (essential), Amboss (supplementary)</li>
          <li>Review Books: Step-Up to Medicine, Master the Boards</li>
          <li>Clinical Guidelines: USPSTF, AHA/ACC, ACOG, AAP guidelines</li>
          <li>High-Yield Recalls: MedRecalls Step 2 CK Premium Package</li>
        </ul>
        
        <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
        <p>With Step 2 CK now being the primary scored USMLE exam, strategic preparation is more important than ever. Focus your efforts on these high-yield topics while developing strong clinical reasoning skills through active question practice.</p>
        
        <p>Remember that Step 2 CK is fundamentally a test of clinical judgment—focus on understanding the "why" behind management decisions rather than simply memorizing facts.</p>
        
        <div class="cta-box">
          <h3>Ready to Master Step 2 CK?</h3>
          <p>Get our comprehensive Step 2 CK recall package featuring 3,000+ verified high-yield questions from recent exams, detailed explanations, and clinical algorithms.</p>
          <a href="https://gumroad.com" class="btn-accent">Get Step 2 CK Premium Package</a>
        </div>
        `
      }
    }
  ];
}

const { slug } = Astro.params;
const { title, date, author, authorTitle, category, image, content } = Astro.props;

import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title={title} description={title}>
  <article class="py-10">
    <div class="container-custom max-w-4xl">
      <!-- Hero -->
      <div class="mb-8">
        <div class="animate-on-scroll">
          <a href="/blog" class="inline-flex items-center text-primary-600 hover:text-primary-700 mb-4">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-4 w-4 mr-2" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 19l-7-7m0 0l7-7m-7 7h18" />
            </svg>
            Back to Blog
          </a>
          
          <span class="inline-block px-3 py-1 text-sm font-semibold bg-accent-500 text-white rounded-full mb-6">
            {category}
          </span>
          
          <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-6">{title}</h1>
          
          <div class="flex items-center mb-8">
            <div class="mr-4">
              <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full overflow-hidden">
                <img src={image} alt={author} class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="font-semibold">{author}</p>
              <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">{authorTitle}</p>
